<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christmas truce</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> truce was a series of widespread unofficial ceasefires, mainly along the Western Front of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> around Christmas in 1914. Roughly 100,000 British and German troops were involved. In some areas men from both sides went into no man's land on Christmas Eve and Christmas Day to exchange food and souvenirs; there were joint burial ceremonies, carols and games of football, while hostilities continued in other sectors. Pope Benedict XV had begged the warring governments for an official truce on 7 December 1914, which both sides refused. The following year only a few units arranged ceasefires, in part because of orders from commanders prohibiting truces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-truce.jpg" alt="Christmas truce" width="330" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1914-es_karácsonyi_fegyverszünet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Christmas truce</b> of 1914 was a series of widespread unofficial ceasefires, mainly along the Western Front of the First World War, involving roughly 100,000 British and German troops.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Christmas truce</b> of 1914, roughly 100,000 British and German troops took part in unofficial ceasefires along the Western Front, with joint burial ceremonies, carols and games of football in some sectors while hostilities continued in others.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
